"},{"box":[55,87,4,28,18,0.9],"m":"gravel"},{"box":[56,80,0,40,8,0.9],"m":"wood"},{"box":[95,-10,0,2,2,7],"m":"dark"},{"box":[80,10,0,2,2,3],"m":"dark"},{"box":[110,40,0,2,2,4],"m":"dark"}],"notes":"Louisiana at Humleb\u00e6k, the line of pavilions read off the ground file (7 m cells) and the rest from published descriptions; no photographs were available. The 1855 villa by Gammel Strandvej, white under a slate roof, is the entrance; J\u00f8rgen Bo and Vilhelm Wohlert's low white pavilions and glass corridors (1958 onward) run north to the wing over the lake, with the Giacometti room, stepping down toward the \u00d8resund, and south down a long glazed corridor to the half-sunk south wing, the concert hall and the children's wing, and the caf\u00e9 over the sea; flat gravel roofs over dark timber fascias, glass toward the park; old beeches, sculpture on the lawn sloping to the sound, woods round it. Guessed: all the wing outlines and heights (4-6.5 m), the lake's shape, where the caf\u00e9 is; the ground falls to the sea but the plate is flat."}